WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.300
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.008
ROGER MARTINEZ: Do
you have a large fleet

00:00:02.008 --> 00:00:03.540
of staple virtual machines?

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:05.762
How many of each operating
system are there?

00:00:05.762 --> 00:00:08.220
Which ones are fully patched
and up-to-date with the latest

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:09.930
security patches?

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:11.700
And can you be sure
that all of them

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.490
have the right software
installed and running?

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:16.079
For a lot of teams,
it's critical

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:18.240
that they're able to answer
all of these questions.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:19.843
But that's not always
easy, especially

00:00:19.843 --> 00:00:21.510
if your infrastructure
includes hundreds

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:24.960
of virtual machines running
various operating systems.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:28.020
Today, I'm going to show you
how VM Manager can add clarity

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:31.440
to those questions and lift some
of those operational burdens

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.990
that come with managing a large
number of virtual machines

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:35.280
in the cloud.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.260
VM Manager is a
suite of tools that

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.450
help you manage the operating
systems of your fleet

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:40.800
of virtual machines.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.410
These capabilities
are nothing new.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:44.910
There are plenty of third-party
tools and services out there

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.430
that help you do some of these
things across your fleet.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:49.970
The advantage of VM Manager
is that it's cloud-native

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:52.470
and works out-of-the-box with
the latest Google Cloud images

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:55.110
across Windows and
Linux operating systems.

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:57.900
Its functionality is accessible
via the Google Cloud Console

00:00:57.900 --> 00:00:59.370
that you're already
familiar with,

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:02.700
or via the gcloud command-line
interface and API.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:05.190
VM Manager works by
periodically communicating

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:07.770
with the google-osconfig
agent that runs on each

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:09.310
of your virtual machines.

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:11.700
The data it receives
helps it serve three core

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.140
functionalities--

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:15.540
durability with OS
inventory management,

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:19.320
security with OS patch
management, and compliance

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.280
with OS configuration
management.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.870
So let's talk about the first
VM Manager feature, OS inventory

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:26.340
management.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.920
OS inventory management
lets you collect and review

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:32.190
operating system information
from your fleet of VMs.

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:34.830
This information includes
operating system version,

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:37.920
packages installed on each
VM, package updates available,

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:40.020
and vulnerability reports.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:42.210
The osconfig agent
that periodically

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:44.340
reports in with the
latest data interfaces

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.650
with standard package
managers, like apt,

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:50.850
yum, and googet to collect
info on packages and updates.

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:52.890
So with OS inventory
management, I

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:55.510
can list all of the
VMs in my project.

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:58.770
And if I want, I can filter
that list by operating system,

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:01.590
by operating system version,
by installed packages,

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:05.310
by missing packages, or
even by pending updates.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:08.250
Next, we have OS
patch management.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:10.650
While OS inventory
management will tell you

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:13.410
what's installed where and
which updates are pending,

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:14.910
OS patch management
is going to let

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:18.210
you install packages and updates
through patch deployment jobs.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:20.190
The OS Patch
Management Dashboard

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:21.660
will help you
visualize your fleet

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:24.420
and identify how many are
ready for updates, broken down

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:26.250
nicely by operating system.

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.050
The dashboard helps
you clearly identify

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:29.910
the machines in your fleet
that have important security

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:31.800
updates, so you'll
never miss a beat when

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:33.390
keeping the machines secure.

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:35.040
When you're ready
to deploy a patch,

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:36.870
you can create a
patch deployment job

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:39.670
to run immediately or
in the future, once

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:41.130
or on a recurring basis.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:43.560
If a patch deployment
fails for whatever reason,

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.480
since VM Manager
is cloud-native,

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:48.120
you'll be able to troubleshoot
it right from the Cloud Console

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:49.290
through Cloud Logging.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.180
The third big
piece of VM Manager

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:53.250
is OS configuration management.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:54.975
With OS configuration
management,

00:02:54.975 --> 00:02:57.600
you can automate the deployment,
configuration, and maintenance

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:00.600
of your virtual machines
from a centralized place.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:03.150
You do this by creating
OS policies that

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:05.760
contain declarative
configuration for each VM,

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:07.710
like packages that
should be present,

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:09.870
package repositories
that should be added,

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:13.380
files that should exist, and
scripts that you'd like to run.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:15.000
A single OS policy
can be written

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:16.890
to write a multi-step workflow.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:19.110
For example, setting
up a repository,

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:22.948
installing a package, and
then changing config files.

00:03:22.948 --> 00:03:24.740
You can specify how to
apply these policies

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:26.390
across your fleet
by using filters

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:31.030
based on labels, OS families,
or even regions and zones.

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:33.040
Once policies are
written and applied,

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:35.020
VM Manager will
constantly check to make

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:36.880
sure the policy is enforced.

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.130
It can also generate
a compliance report

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:41.332
that will tell you if any
machines have fallen out

00:03:41.332 --> 00:03:42.790
of compliance with
the requirements

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:44.060
that you specified.

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:47.200
If you want to use VM Manager
with your fleet, here's how.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:49.540
First, there are two APIs
that you'll want to enable

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:53.860
or your project, OS Config
and Container Analysis.

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:57.130
Then you need to enable OS
Config on each VM in your fleet

00:03:57.130 --> 00:04:01.540
by setting the metadata tag
enable-osconfig to TRUE.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:03.400
This will make
the osconfig agent

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:06.040
active on the
corresponding instances.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:08.080
You also have the option
of adding this metadata

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:11.380
tag to your project so
that all VMs are included.

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:15.070
Each VM will also have to have
an attached service account.

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:18.350
It'll be used to sign
requests to the OS Config API.

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:20.200
And finally, each
VM in your fleet

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:23.410
is going to have to have the
osconfig agent installed.

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:25.120
The good news is
that this is already

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:27.580
included on the latest
OS images available.

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:29.470
If it isn't installed
on your machines,

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:31.390
check out the documentation
in the description

00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:34.240
below for steps on how to get
it installed on each operating

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:34.930
system.

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:38.380
Let's check out VM Manager with
a quick walkthrough of OS patch

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:39.800
management.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:41.390
What I have is a
Google Cloud project

00:04:41.390 --> 00:04:44.600
with 17 virtual machines running
various operating systems.

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:47.830
For this demonstration, I have
the patch job executor IAM

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:48.607
role assigned.

00:04:48.607 --> 00:04:50.690
That will give me permission
to create and execute

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:52.950
patch jobs in the project.

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:56.540
First, let's take a look
at the OS Patch Dashboard.

00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:59.060
From here, we can see
our VMs broken down

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:00.300
by operating system.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.270
We also see a chart for
each operating system,

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:04.970
showing us the virtual
machines by available updates.

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:07.850
If we click View Details
on any of those charts,

00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:11.330
we'll get a list view
of our virtual machines.

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:13.250
Up here, we'll be able
to filter the list

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:15.210
by a few different fields.

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:19.550
For example, by adding a
Filter on OS Distribution,

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:22.250
we can view all CentOS
and Red Hat machines

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:26.023
in a single list with their
Zone, OS info, and Status.

00:05:26.023 --> 00:05:27.440
If we want to get
more information

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.300
about a single machine's
available updates,

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:32.660
we can simply click the name
and get some more details.

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:36.440
Under Basic info, we once
again see the OS information

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:39.380
and a summary of its
available updates.

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:41.930
As you can see down here,
there are quite a few updates

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:43.700
with different Classifications.

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:48.200
Let's add a filter to see only
updates classified as Critical.

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:51.140
That narrows things down
to three critical updates.

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:53.630
If you want more information
about an individual update,

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:55.520
check out the CVE column.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:57.560
You'll be able to go
straight to the CVE website

00:05:57.560 --> 00:05:59.870
to learn more about the
vulnerability each update will

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:00.740
resolve.

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:02.360
Since these are
critical updates,

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:06.190
let's install these updates
by creating a patch job.

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:09.040
We can do that right here
by selecting the updates

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:10.990
and clicking Apply Update.

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:14.420
Creating a new patch deployment
happens with five steps.

00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:16.990
First, let's select our VMs.

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:19.570
Our one CentOS machine is
automatically selected,

00:06:19.570 --> 00:06:22.940
but we can add more by
adjusting the filter

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:25.400
and selecting all
CentOS machines.

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:27.413
Next is the Patch configuration.

00:06:27.413 --> 00:06:28.580
First, we'll give it a name.

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:31.430
Since we initiated this straight
from the VM Details panel,

00:06:31.430 --> 00:06:33.800
the packages we want to
update are already here

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:35.570
under Include packages.

00:06:35.570 --> 00:06:37.820
We can move on to step
three, scheduling.

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:39.290
Here, we simply
indicate whether we

00:06:39.290 --> 00:06:42.710
want to roll this deployment out
now or sometime in the future.

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:45.320
We also can create this
rollout on a recurring schedule

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:47.683
for packages that we know
we want to update regularly.

00:06:47.683 --> 00:06:49.100
And the Duration
field will let us

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:50.990
specify how long
before the deployment

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:52.340
is considered a failure.

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:54.860
Step four brings us a
few more rollout options

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:57.900
that let us control how quickly
you want to roll it out.

00:06:57.900 --> 00:06:59.640
The Threshold and
the Percentage will

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:01.950
help to slow down the
rollout so that we don't have

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:03.910
to patch all machines at once.

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:06.510
And finally, step five
is Advanced options,

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:09.090
where we can provide
VM Manager with any pre

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:11.640
or post-patch scripts that
we'd like to execute before

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:13.170
or after the patch completes.

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:16.950
Additionally, here, there are
some Post-patch reboot options.

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:18.660
Once we finish
the configuration,

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:21.600
we can click Deploy to kick
off this patch deployment job.

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:24.900
Once it starts, you can click
the job name on the Patch Jobs

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:28.110
page to get details
about its execution.

00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:31.570
The Update info page will recap
what this job will be doing.

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:34.320
And on the bottom, we can see
the status of each VM included

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:35.850
in this patch deployment.

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:38.400
Once finished, each VM
will have the status

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:41.040
of Success or Failure,
along with a link

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:43.740
to view logs associated
with that patch job.

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:46.260
That was just a simple
example of OS patch management

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:49.350
in action and only a small peek
at what VM Manager as a whole

00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:50.850
can do.

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:53.033
OS inventory management is
going to give you insight

00:07:53.033 --> 00:07:54.450
into the operating
systems running

00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:57.570
on your virtual machines in
your Google Cloud projects.

00:07:57.570 --> 00:08:00.300
OS patch management will make
sure the right packages are

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:03.240
kept up-to-date and
free of vulnerabilities.

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:05.178
And OS configuration
management will

00:08:05.178 --> 00:08:07.470
automate the deployment,
configuration, and maintenance

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:09.180
of your virtual machines--

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:12.300
all of this straight
from the Cloud Console.

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:14.250
For more information
on VM Manager,

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:16.020
check out the link
to the documentation

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:17.620
in the description below.

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:18.540
Thanks for watching.

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:21.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

